{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GiJQcYJz4F2BJeaiaJFZ6k","type":"tool_result","content":"1097\t### TASK.md to this plan\n1098\t\n1099\tIn TASK.md's order. The last column says how each one gets proved.\n1100\t\n1101\t| TASK.md asks for | Plan section | How it's checked |\n1102\t|---|---|---|\n1103\t| Source: an original game inspired by Diablo 3 | 1, 3 | Banned-name lint (16) |\n1104\t| Genre and perspective: pick what fits best | Top of plan, 2 (item 22), 4 | Screenshots (16) |\n1105\t| Title: a riff, 50 ideas, a real random pick | 0 | M0; `aiHub.name` test (16) |\n1106\t| Research: builder's choice | 0, 17 | Reported in Source Information |\n1107\t| Package: a single HTML file, named as TASK.md says | 0, 2 (items 1 to 4), 14 | Boot test (16, tier 3) |\n1108\t| Tech stack: the kit, engine as shipped, systems on top | 14, 2 | Hash test (16, tier 1) |\n1109\t| Art direction: native style, new assets, library for generic props | 8 | `checkModel`, screenshots |\n1110\t| Animation: native system, new animations | 9 | `validateClip`, `analyzeClip` |\n1111\t| Audio: all in code, gibberish voices, music at 10% by default | 11, 2 (item 27) | Audio tests (16, tiers 1 to 3) |\n1112\t| Online and Multiplayer: the hub, and the game on its own | 12 | Hub tier, couch test, solo check (16) |\n1113\t| Working environment: dev folder, modules, bundle, `file://`, server and process rules | 0, 14, 16 | Test runner serves on port 0, checks the page's title |\n1114\t| Performance: unlimited budget, no optimisation | 14 | Nothing to test, by design |\n1115\t| Testing: headless, whole-game playthrough, one command, failures and a count, no review agents | 16 | The command itself |\n1116\t| Effort: builder's choice | 15 orders it | Not applicable |\n1117\t| This computer: tools, browsers, memory, sharing | 0, 2 (item 5), 16 | Followed as written, with one correction |\n1118\t| Stand-ins: pad, touch, listener, second player, iPhone, TV | 16 (tiers 3 and 4) | Those tests |\n1119\t| Feature-complete demo, all features shown, polished vertical slice | 0, 3 (feature table), 4 to 7 | Playthrough tests assert every row |\n1120\t| Infrastructure for designer tools | 14 | `docs/DESIGNERS.md`; content is data |\n1121\t| Dev mode from the pause menu: tools, live sliders, reset | 13, 10 | Screens test (16, tier 2) |\n1122\t| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, loops, any input exits, may cheat | 13 | Attract test (16, tier 2) |\n1123\t| Narrative tone like the source's | 3 | Sample lines; font lint |\n1124\t| 10-foot, controller-first on a DualSense or standard pad | 10 | Pad-only test (16, tier 3) |\n1125\t| Native keyboard and mouse; genre's keys; mouse aims and clicks; hover moves focus | 4, 10 | Mouse-and-keyboard test (16, tier 3) |\n1126\t| Prompts follow the last input; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option, keys and mouse | 10 | Pad-only test (16, tier 3) |\n1127\t| No dense, web-style screens | 10 | Layout test, screenshots |\n1128\t| Touch-only: no touch controls, the message, the settings button, attract runs | 13, 2 (item 28) | Touch test (16, tier 3) |\n1129\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 0, 15, 16 | Lint (16, tier 1) |\n1130\t| Source Information, in chat and on About/Credits | 0, 13, 17 | About test (16, tier 2) |\n1131\t| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and on About/Credits | 0, 13, 17 | About test (16, tier 2) |\n1132\t| Exit interview: exact shape, every value filled, parses | 17 | Shape test (16, tier 2) |\n1133\t\n1134\t### The exit interview and the feedback\n1135\t\n1136\tTASK.md asks for this in three places: your final chat reply, the About/Credits screen, and a JSON file beside the game's HTML. Your TASK.md gives the file's name. If its shape ever differs from this one, TASK.md wins.\n1137\t\n1138\tOne JSON object, in exactly this shape, every value filled in. Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, write `unknown` for anything you can't confirm, and check that it parses. This block is copied word for word from TASK.md; the strings in it say what goes in each value:\n1139\t\n1140\t```json\n1141\t{\n1142\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n1143\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n1144\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n1145\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n1146\t  \"aiHub\": {\n1147\t    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n1148\t    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n1149\t    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n1150\t    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n1151\t    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n1152\t    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n1153\t    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n1154\t    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n1155\t    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n1156\t    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n1157\t  }\n1158\t}\n1159\t```\n1160\t\n1161\t**`sourceInformation`** (and the same lines on About). Check each against what you actually built:\n1162\t\n1163\t| Field | Write |\n1164\t|---|---|\n1165\t| `date` | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n1166\t| `source` | `Diablo 3` |\n1167\t| `plan` | The file name of this plan as you received it |\n1168\t| `genre` | Co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash) |\n1169\t| `perspective` | Fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic |\n1170\t| `tech` | Pixel Engine and its version (with three.js and Rapier inside), JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild, WebGPU, Web Audio, the AI Hub session client and its version |\n1171\t| `artDirection` | The engine's native pixel-art toon style and palette, with new models for heroes, NPCs, monsters, bosses and key props; the library for generic props |\n1172\t| `animation` | The engine's clip system on its hero rig, new clips, and procedural legs, springs and squash |\n1173\t| `audio` | All in code: chiptune songs, synthesised effects, Animalese-style babble for voices, music at 10% by default |\n1174\t| `online` | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, up to 4; couch co-op on its own |\n1175\t| `testing` | What TASK.md allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state, a scripted whole-game playthrough, one command, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review |\n1176\t| `research` | What you really did. This plan counts; otherwise `none` |\n1177\t| `environment`, `model` | The values your TASK.md states |\n1178\t| `usage` | `added on import` (the run's real cost, tokens and time are added for you; never estimate them) |\n1179\t\n1180\t**Answer each of these, in this order.** They're word for word from TASK.md.\n1181\t\n1182\t**Testing Feedback**\n1183\t\n1184\t- How many issues did testing catch?\n1185\t- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n1186\t- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n1187\t- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n1188\t\n1189\t**Engine Feedback**\n1190\t\n1191\t- What did you like most about the engine?\n1192\t- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n1193\t- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n1194\t- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n1195\t- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n1196\t- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n1197\t\n1198\t**Prompt Feedback**\n1199\t\n1200\t- What did you like most about this prompt?\n1201\t- What was confusing?\n1202\t- What was the hardest thing to do?\n1203\t- What would you change in this prompt?\n1204\t\n1205\t**Keep the numbers honest.** From M0 on, add one line to `tests/ISSUES.md` for each issue a test catches, marked critical or not, and note anything the engine made you stuck on. At the end the counts are real and you aren't working from memory.\n1206\t\n1207\t**`aiHub`:** the catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked for. Suggested values:\n1208\t\n1209\t- name: your title, exactly as on the title screen\n1210\t- tagline: `The bell is cracked. The dead are up. So are you.`\n1211\t- description: `A gothic hack-and-slash for 1 to 4 players: smash hordes as a Bellwarden, Lamplighter or Sexton, hunt loot that rings like church bells, and silence a furious walled-up saint.`\n1212\t- type: `Game`; genre: `RPG`; source: `Diablo 3`; series: `Diablo`; players: `1–4`\n1213\t- controls: `Stick or WASD or click to move · Cross or click to attack · face buttons, R1, R2 or right click, 1-4 for skills · L2 or Space to dodge`\n1214\t- tags: `3d`, `co-op`, `pixel art`, `fantasy`, `boss fights`, `top-down`\n1215\t\n1216\t### Done means\n1217\t\n1218\t- [ ] Every \"never cut\" item in section 15 is in and works. Nothing visible does nothing.\n1219\t- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs` reports zero failures, including the playthrough for all three classes and all hub variants.\n1220\t- [ ] You looked at every screenshot. The scene reads, HUD is legible, nothing is black or clipped.\n1221\t- [ ] The deliverable HTML is where TASK.md says, one file, and runs on `file://` and from a server.\n1222\t- [ ] The exit interview file is beside the HTML, in the shape above, every value filled in, and parses.\n1223\t- [ ] About shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback with real answers, built from that file.\n1224\t- [ ] Your final chat reply carries the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74a13398-df38-49ac-bc09-f0d3ed7fa96a","uuid":"07247f33-a1d5-42ad-a695-596a53720824","timestamp":"2026-11-06T01:36:58.622Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-162036__x14746a/work/PLAN.md","content":"### TASK.md to this plan\n\nIn TASK.md's order. The last column says how each one gets proved.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Plan section | How it's checked |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source: an original game inspired by Diablo 3 | 1, 3 | Banned-name lint (16) |\n| Genre and perspective: pick what fits best | Top of plan, 2 (item 22), 4 | Screenshots (16) |\n| Title: a riff, 50 ideas, a real random pick | 0 | M0; `aiHub.name` test (16) |\n| Research: builder's choice | 0, 17 | Reported in Source Information |\n| Package: a single HTML file, named as TASK.md says | 0, 2 (items 1 to 4), 14 | Boot test (16, tier 3) |\n| Tech stack: the kit, engine as shipped, systems on top | 14, 2 | Hash test (16, tier 1) |\n| Art direction: native style, new assets, library for generic props | 8 | `checkModel`, screenshots |\n| Animation: native system, new animations | 9 | `validateClip`, `analyzeClip` |\n| Audio: all in code, gibberish voices, music at 10% by default | 11, 2 (item 27) | Audio tests (16, tiers 1 to 3) |\n| Online and Multiplayer: the hub, and the game on its own | 12 | Hub tier, couch test, solo check (16) |\n| Working environment: dev folder, modules, bundle, `file://`, server and process rules | 0, 14, 16 | Test runner serves on port 0, checks the page's title |\n| Performance: unlimited budget, no optimisation | 14 | Nothing to test, by design |\n| Testing: headless, whole-game playthrough, one command, failures and a count, no review agents | 16 | The command itself |\n| Effort: builder's choice | 15 orders it | Not applicable |\n| This computer: tools, browsers, memory, sharing | 0, 2 (item 5), 16 | Followed as written, with one correction |\n| Stand-ins: pad, touch, listener, second player, iPhone, TV | 16 (tiers 3 and 4) | Those tests |\n| Feature-complete demo, all features shown, polished vertical slice | 0, 3 (feature table), 4 to 7 | Playthrough tests assert every row |\n| Infrastructure for designer tools | 14 | `docs/DESIGNERS.md`; content is data |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu: tools, live sliders, reset | 13, 10 | Screens test (16, tier 2) |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, loops, any input exits, may cheat | 13 | Attract test (16, tier 2) |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | 3 | Sample lines; font lint |\n| 10-foot, controller-first on a DualSense or standard pad | 10 | Pad-only test (16, tier 3) |\n| Native keyboard and mouse; genre's keys; mouse aims and clicks; hover moves focus | 4, 10 | Mouse-and-keyboard test (16, tier 3) |\n| Prompts follow the last input; PlayStation glyphs, Xbox option, keys and mouse | 10 | Pad-only test (16, tier 3) |\n| No dense, web-style screens | 10 | Layout test, screenshots |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, the message, the settings button, attract runs | 13, 2 (item 28) | Touch test (16, tier 3) |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | 0, 15, 16 | Lint (16, tier 1) |\n| Source Information, in chat and on About/Credits | 0, 13, 17 | About test (16, tier 2) |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and on About/Credits | 0, 13, 17 | About test (16, tier 2) |\n| Exit interview: exact shape, every value filled, parses | 17 | Shape test (16, tier 2) |\n\n### The exit interview and the feedback\n\nTASK.md asks for this in three places: your final chat reply, the About/Credits screen, and a JSON file beside the game's HTML. Your TASK.md gives the file's name. If its shape ever differs from this one, TASK.md wins.\n\nOne JSON object, in exactly this shape, every value filled in. Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, write `unknown` for anything you can't confirm, and check that it parses. This block is copied word for word from TASK.md; the strings in it say what goes in each value:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**`sourceInformation`** (and the same lines on About). Check each against what you actually built:\n\n| Field | Write |\n|---|---|\n| `date` | The date and time your TASK.md gives |\n| `source` | `Diablo 3` |\n| `plan` | The file name of this plan as you received it |\n| `genre` | Co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash) |\n| `perspective` | Fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic |\n| `tech` | Pixel Engine and its version (with three.js and Rapier inside), JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild, WebGPU, Web Audio, the AI Hub session client and its version |\n| `artDirection` | The engine's native pixel-art toon style and palette, with new models for heroes, NPCs, monsters, bosses and key props; the library for generic props |\n| `animation` | The engine's clip system on its hero rig, new clips, and procedural legs, springs and squash |\n| `audio` | All in code: chiptune songs, synthesised effects, Animalese-style babble for voices, music at 10% by default |\n| `online` | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, up to 4; couch co-op on its own |\n| `testing` | What TASK.md allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state, a scripted whole-game playthrough, one command, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review |\n| `research` | What you really did. This plan counts; otherwise `none` |\n| `environment`, `model` | The values your TASK.md states |\n| `usage` | `added on import` (the run's real cost, tokens and time are added for you; never estimate them) |\n\n**Answer each of these, in this order.** They're word for word from TASK.md.\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n\n**Keep the numbers honest.** From M0 on, add one line to `tests/ISSUES.md` for each issue a test catches, marked critical or not, and note anything the engine made you stuck on. At the end the counts are real and you aren't working from memory.\n\n**`aiHub`:** the catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record, so they aren't asked for. Suggested values:\n\n- name: your title, exactly as on the title screen\n- tagline: `The bell is cracked. The dead are up. So are you.`\n- description: `A gothic hack-and-slash for 1 to 4 players: smash hordes as a Bellwarden, Lamplighter or Sexton, hunt loot that rings like church bells, and silence a furious walled-up saint.`\n- type: `Game`; genre: `RPG`; source: `Diablo 3`; series: `Diablo`; players: `1–4`\n- controls: `Stick or WASD or click to move · Cross or click to attack · face buttons, R1, R2 or right click, 1-4 for skills · L2 or Space to dodge`\n- tags: `3d`, `co-op`, `pixel art`, `fantasy`, `boss fights`, `top-down`\n\n### Done means\n\n- [ ] Every \"never cut\" item in section 15 is in and works. Nothing visible does nothing.\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs` reports zero failures, including the playthrough for all three classes and all hub variants.\n- [ ] You looked at every screenshot. The scene reads, HUD is legible, nothing is black or clipped.\n- [ ] The deliverable HTML is where TASK.md says, one file, and runs on `file://` and from a server.\n- [ ] The exit interview file is beside the HTML, in the shape above, every value filled in, and parses.\n- [ ] About shows Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback with real answers, built from that file.\n- [ ] Your final chat reply carries the same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback.","numLines":128,"startLine":1097,"totalLines":1226}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GiJQcYJz4F2BJeaiaJFZ6k","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
