{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkrcMM3og34z44jFFae3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Diabellical is finished and both hand-ins are in the work folder.** `node tools/test.mjs` reports 59 passed, 0 failed on the final build, including a whole-game playthrough for each of the three classes and all five AI Hub test setups.\n\nAfter that run I only deleted my scratch files and the kit downloads, then reran the sim tier, which passed (25 of 25). Nothing was committed, since you didn't ask for that.\n\nThere are limits to what this computer could check:\n- **Controllers, phones and second players:** these used stand-ins (stub pads, Pixel and tablet profiles, two browser pages).\n- **Sound:** checked by measuring Web Audio levels, not by listening.\n- **TV view:** judged from 1920×1080 screenshots.\n- **Speed:** frame rate wasn't measured, as TASK.md puts that out of scope.\n\nThe dev folder is `Diabellical-Diablo3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162036/`, with tests, tools and `docs/DESIGNERS.md`. The same answers below are on the in-game About screen and in `exit-interview.json`.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 16:20:36\n- **Source:** Diablo 3\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Co-op action RPG (hack-and-slash); fixed 3/4 isometric, orthographic\n- **Tech:** Pixel Engine 0.46.0 (with three.js r186 and Rapier inside), JavaScript modules bundled with esbuild 0.28, WebGPU, Web Audio, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native pixel-art toon style and palette, with new models for heroes, NPCs, monsters, bosses and key props; the library for generic props\n- **Animation:** The engine's clip system on its hero rig, new clips, and procedural legs, springs and squash\n- **Audio:** All in code: chiptune songs, synthesised effects, Animalese-style babble for voices, music at 10% by default\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared-world, up to 4; couch co-op on its own\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts that play the game and check its state, a scripted whole-game playthrough, one command, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan: PLAN.md's own notes on the source were used and no further research was done\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 26. Each one is a line in tests/ISSUES.md: 17 from the scripted tests and 9 from screenshot runs.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 10. They include The Way Home spawning inside a wall (a Knell Run with no exit), the Bursar's Foreclosure walls locking a hero out of the fight, blinks through walls, a missing door to the Drowned Pantry, heroes stuck in walls, and a physics panic in long sessions.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The whole-game playthrough, once per class, played by a bot through the real menus. It found the stuck heroes, the missing Pantry door and a Knell Run left alive in town, none of which a unit test would have reached. Its sibling, The Last Bell run, found the walled-in exit on the final pass.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The tuning check that defaults round-trip and every slider has a home. It never caught anything, because that code hardly changed after it was written.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** The headless engine. Engine.headless and engine.step let one test file play the entire campaign through the real game object with no browser, in about a minute per class. Second place goes to models and clips as plain data: every hero, monster and boss here is data the engine's own checker can lint.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times. Rapier panicked with 'wasm unreachable' deep into long sessions, traced to Breakables, and I never reproduced it in isolation, so I replaced those shatters with my own ballistic chunks rather than guess further. Trail produced NaN vertices when given two identical points, which took a NaN scan of the scene to find. I also guessed wrong about the gaitClip lift parameter, which made feet slip until the clip analyser pointed at it.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The sim and netcode split: a deterministic plain-JS world (heroes, monsters, skills as data, loot, quests, Knell runs) that a bot, couch seats, and a hub host all drive the same way, with a compact state stream for hub clients. Close behind: the immediate-mode 10-foot menu system with focus, glyph prompts and mouse hover on the engine's pixel HUD.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting an RPG's words into a HUD only 430 to 480 art pixels wide with one font and no alpha. Item names, skill text and toasts all fight for the same few pixels, and the HUD draws straight over everything. Debugging through the bundled engine was the second hardest part, because errors surface as minified frames.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** none\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Make Breakables and Trail survive bad input: skip degenerate points and retire debris safely, so a long session cannot panic the physics world. A close second is real layering with translucency in the HUD.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** It says exactly what done means and how it will be checked: the single file, the exit interview shape, the attract rule, the touch rule, and one test command. The plan's verified engine facts meant I could trust details instead of rediscovering them.\n- **What was confusing?** Mostly small things: the plan and TASK.md both speak to testing and hand-ins, so I had to remember that TASK.md wins. \"Use screenshots only for what a script can't check\" sits beside the plan's \"look at every screenshot\", which took a moment to reconcile.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Scope. Three classes with skill runes, loot, a campaign with bosses, an endgame, couch and online co-op, attract, dev tools and a full shell all have to work together, and each one multiplies the test surface. Keeping online clients in agreement with the host while the sim kept changing was the hardest single thread.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Add a short priority list for what to cut first if time runs short, and name the HUD's real width up front so text budgets are planned from the start. Otherwise I would keep it as it is.\n\nFiles are in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-162036__x14746a/work/`:\n- `Diabellical-Diablo3-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-162036.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":307,"cache_read_input_tokens":173358,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":307},"output_tokens":788,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"74a13398-df38-49ac-bc09-f0d3ed7fa96a","uuid":"1e11def7-92b2-4ac1-a8d3-9882b94dfba5","timestamp":"2026-11-06T02:06:16.887Z","request_id":"req_011CgkrcM4w1yPt2pa9TWs53"}
